{
  "image_name": "4daced463267e4c4f4c1bdcc23d3382e.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the dual role of the complement system in cancer, contrasting antitumour and protumour effects. On the left, antibody–antigen binding on tumour cells triggers classical complement activation through the C1 complex. This leads to deposition of C3b/iC3b on the cell surface, formation of the membrane attack complex (MAC), and release of C3a and C5a. These events support tumour cell killing in several ways: direct cell lysis by MAC, phagocytosis through Fc receptors and CR3, and enhanced natural killer cell activity with perforin and granzymes. The overall message is that complement can cooperate with antibodies to destroy cancer cells.\n\nOn the right, the same system is shown in a deregulated state within the tumour microenvironment, where complement activation instead promotes cancer progression. Cancer cells are coated with C3b and surrounded by C3a and C5a, which are linked to tumour cell proliferation, invasion, and metastasis. These signals also reshape surrounding stromal and immune cells: fibroblasts and dendritic cells are altered, protumour neutrophils are recruited, effector T-cell responses are suppressed, macrophages shift toward an M2-like state, and MDSCs are recruited. Overall, the image emphasizes complement as a context-dependent system that can either fight tumours or help them grow.",
